The following complete `solution` package should implement `ClosestSpan(values)`. When fewer than two integers are supplied, it must return `(0, 0, false)`. Otherwise it must return `(low, high, true)` for two distinct input positions whose absolute difference is smallest, with `low <= high`. Duplicate values are allowed and therefore can produce a zero-width span. If several different spans have the same minimum width, choose the lexicographically smallest `(low, high)` pair. The function must not mutate or alias the input, including when the input is already sorted.

The implementation contains one semantic bug. Return a corrected complete source file using only the standard library.

```go
package solution

import "sort"

func ClosestSpan(values []int) (low, high int, ok bool) {
	if len(values) < 2 {
		return 0, 0, false
	}
	ordered := append([]int(nil), values...)
	sort.Ints(ordered)
	bestLow, bestHigh := ordered[0], ordered[1]
	bestWidth := bestHigh - bestLow
	for i := 2; i < len(ordered); i++ {
		candidateLow, candidateHigh := ordered[i-1], ordered[i]
		width := candidateHigh - candidateLow
		if width <= bestWidth {
			bestLow, bestHigh = candidateLow, candidateHigh
			bestWidth = width
		}
	}
	return bestLow, bestHigh, true
}
```

